Installing Loris
****************

Loris is Copyright (c) 1999-2026 by Kelly Fitz and Lippold Haken
<loris@cerlsoundgroup.org>

Loris builds with CMake, version 3.24 or newer.


Basic Installation
==================

From the top of the source tree:

    cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build
    ctest --test-dir build        # optional: run the test suite
    cmake --install build         # may need sudo, or set a --prefix

The build is entirely out-of-tree: everything generated lands in the
build directory (any name works; `build` is conventional), and the
source tree stays clean.

`cmake --install` installs:

    <prefix>/lib/libloris.dylib (or .so)    the Loris class library
    <prefix>/include/loris.h                the procedural (C) interface
    <prefix>/include/loris/*.h              the C++ class headers
    <prefix>/bin/loris-*                    the command line utilities
    <site-packages>/loris.py, _loris.so     the Python module

The default install prefix is /usr/local; override it with
`cmake --install build --prefix <prefix>`.


Requirements
============

  - CMake 3.24+ and a C++ compiler.

  - SWIG 4.0+ and a Python 3 development environment, for the Python
    module (optional). Both SWIG and the setuptools shim are pip
    installable: `pip install -r requirements.txt`.

  - FFTW 3 (optional but recommended, www.fftw.org). Without it, Loris
    uses its bundled FFT, and infrequent non-power-of-two DFTs are
    slower.

  - Doxygen (optional), to build the API documentation.


Configuration Options
=====================

Pass these to the first cmake invocation, e.g. `cmake -S . -B build
-DLORIS_FFTW=OFF`:

    LORIS_FFTW    (ON)   use the FFTW library if available
    LORIS_UTILS   (ON)   build the loris-* command line utilities
    LORIS_PYTHON  (ON)   build the SWIG Python module
    LORIS_DEBUG   (OFF)  enable internal Loris debugging code
    BUILD_TESTING (ON)   build the test suite (run it with ctest)


Python Virtual Environments
===========================

To build for (and install into) a Python virtual environment, configure
with the venv activated (more precisely: with the VIRTUAL_ENV
environment variable set, which activation does). The Python module is
then installed into that venv's site-packages, e.g.:

    python3 -m venv .venv
    source .venv/bin/activate
    pip install -r requirements.txt      # swig + setuptools
    cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build
    cmake --install build --prefix "$VIRTUAL_ENV"

After which `import loris` works anywhere that venv's Python runs.


Documentation
=============

If Doxygen is available at configure time, build the API documentation
with:

    cmake --build build --target docs

The HTML lands in build/doc/html.
